---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How It Works — WhatMedia"
  description="Accessible technical explanation of WhatsApp compression behavior and WhatMedia's optimization pipeline."
  lang="en"
  currentPath="/en/how-it-works"
>
  <div class="py-12 md:py-20">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <!-- Breadcrumb -->
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-mono text-wm-muted">
        <a href="/en/" class="hover:text-wm-ink">Home</a>
        <span>/</span>
        <span class="text-wm-signal font-semibold">How it works</span>
      </div>

      <h1
        class="mt-4 font-heading text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-wm-ink sm:text-4xl md:text-5xl"
      >
        How WhatsApp compresses your statuses, and how WhatMedia protects them.
      </h1>

      <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-wm-muted leading-relaxed">
        WhatsApp is not a neutral media player: it is a global messaging network handling billions
        of daily uploads that ruthlessly minimizes server bandwidth costs. Here is the exact
        technical breakdown and our solution.
      </p>

      <!-- Section 1 -->
      <section class="mt-14 space-y-6">
        <h2 class="font-heading text-2xl font-bold text-wm-ink border-b border-wm-border pb-3">
          1. The automatic recompression trap
        </h2>
        <p class="text-base text-wm-muted leading-relaxed">
          When you post a video to your WhatsApp status, the app does not send your raw camera
          capture:
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-6">
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-wm-border bg-wm-surface p-5 space-y-2">
            <span class="font-mono text-xs font-bold text-wm-warn uppercase"
              >Scenario A: Direct Upload</span
            >
            <p class="text-sm text-wm-ink font-semibold">Raw 4K 60 fps HDR video (150 MB)</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-wm-muted">
              WhatsApp passes it through a heavy server-side transcode: downscaled to 720p, bitrate
              crushed to ~1.5 Mbps, and colors clipped due to lack of standard HDR-to-SDR
              conversion. Result: blurry, blotchy, and washed-out.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-xl border border-wm-signal/30 bg-wm-signal-light/30 p-5 space-y-2">
            <span class="font-mono text-xs font-bold text-wm-signal uppercase"
              >Scenario B: With WhatMedia</span
            >
            <p class="text-sm text-wm-ink font-semibold">
              Pre-calibrated 1080p 30 fps SDR video (15 MB)
            </p>
            <p class="text-xs text-wm-muted">
              The file already precisely matches WhatsApp's optimal ingest profile. WhatsApp's
              recompression algorithm triggers minimal degradation. Maximum sharpness and fidelity
              are retained.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2: Four Technical Pillars -->
      <section class="mt-14 space-y-6">
        <h2 class="font-heading text-2xl font-bold text-wm-ink border-b border-wm-border pb-3">
          2. The four pillars of optimization
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <!-- Pillar A -->
          <div class="rounded-wm-card border border-wm-border bg-wm-surface p-6">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
              <span
                class="flex h-8 w-8 items-center justify-center rounded-lg bg-wm-focus-light font-mono text-sm font-bold text-wm-focus"
                >A</span
              >
              <h3 class="font-heading text-lg font-bold text-wm-ink">
                Calibrated Resolution & Bitrate
              </h3>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-wm-muted leading-relaxed">
              We convert videos to 1080×1920 (for 9:16 vertical statuses) or 1920×1080 (landscape)
              encoded with H.264 (High Profile @ Level 4.1). Bitrate is tailored to the highest
              threshold accepted before triggering WhatsApp's severe secondary squash pass.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Pillar B -->
          <div class="rounded-wm-card border border-wm-border bg-wm-surface p-6">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
              <span
                class="flex h-8 w-8 items-center justify-center rounded-lg bg-wm-focus-light font-mono text-sm font-bold text-wm-focus"
                >B</span
              >
              <h3 class="font-heading text-lg font-bold text-wm-ink">
                HDR to SDR Tone Mapping (BT.709)
              </h3>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-wm-muted leading-relaxed">
              Modern smartphones record by default in wide gamut HDR (Dolby Vision / HLG Rec.2020
              10-bit). WhatsApp fails to map these gamuts correctly, producing a milky, faded
              picture. WhatMedia applies precise color transformation to standard BT.709 8-bit SDR,
              keeping colors vibrant and rich.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Pillar C -->
          <div class="rounded-wm-card border border-wm-border bg-wm-surface p-6">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
              <span
                class="flex h-8 w-8 items-center justify-center rounded-lg bg-wm-focus-light font-mono text-sm font-bold text-wm-focus"
                >C</span
              >
              <h3 class="font-heading text-lg font-bold text-wm-ink">
                Constant Frame Rate (30 fps CFR)
              </h3>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-wm-muted leading-relaxed">
              Cameras often produce variable frame rate (VFR) or 60/120 fps streams. WhatsApp
              stutters when processing high-framerate VFR clips. WhatMedia stabilizes footage to a
              constant 30 fps with a fixed 1-second closed GOP (Group of Pictures) for instant,
              stutter-free playback.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Pillar D -->
          <div class="rounded-wm-card border border-wm-border bg-wm-surface p-6">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
              <span
                class="flex h-8 w-8 items-center justify-center rounded-lg bg-wm-focus-light font-mono text-sm font-bold text-wm-focus"
                >D</span
              >
              <h3 class="font-heading text-lg font-bold text-wm-ink">Smart Video Segmentation</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-wm-muted leading-relaxed">
              WhatsApp cuts off videos after 30 or 60 seconds. WhatMedia slices your footage into
              sequential parts ready to post (Part 1/3, 2/3, 3/3). You choose your preferred
              duration (30s, 45s, or 60s): shorter clips yield higher bitrate density per second,
              translating to noticeably crisper visual fidelity.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3: In-Browser Architecture -->
      <section class="mt-14 space-y-6">
        <h2 class="font-heading text-2xl font-bold text-wm-ink border-b border-wm-border pb-3">
          3. Modern Web Tech: 100% In-Browser Execution
        </h2>
        <p class="text-base text-wm-muted leading-relaxed">
          To ensure uncompromising privacy and avoid costly external video servers, WhatMedia relies
          entirely on cutting-edge browser capabilities:
        </p>
        <ul class="space-y-3 text-sm text-wm-muted">
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="font-mono text-wm-signal font-bold">•</span>
            <span
              ><strong>WebCodecs API:</strong> Direct hardware-accelerated video encoding on your device
              GPU (Apple Silicon, Qualcomm Snapdragon, Google Tensor) for lightning-fast exports with
              minimal battery drain.</span
            >
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="font-mono text-wm-signal font-bold">•</span>
            <span
              ><strong>WebAssembly (FFmpeg Wasm):</strong> Reliable fallback processing engine sandboxed
              inside a background Web Worker.</span
            >
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="font-mono text-wm-signal font-bold">•</span>
            <span
              ><strong>Zero Network Data Transfer:</strong> Open developer tools, turn on Airplane Mode:
              WhatMedia functions entirely offline.</span
            >
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- CTA Footer -->
      <div class="mt-16 text-center rounded-2xl border border-wm-border bg-wm-surface p-8">
        <h3 class="font-heading text-2xl font-bold text-wm-ink">Try it with your own videos</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-wm-muted">
          See the difference in your WhatsApp statuses in just 3 clicks.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-6 flex justify-center">
          <a
            href="/app/"
            class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-wm-button bg-wm-signal px-7 py-3 text-sm font-bold text-white shadow-md hover:bg-wm-signal-hover transition-colors"
          >
            <span>Launch WhatMedia</span>
            <svg
              xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
              class="h-4 w-4"
              fill="none"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              stroke="currentColor"
            >
              <path
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                stroke-width="2.5"
                d="M14 5l7 7m0 0l-7 7m7-7H3"></path>
            </svg>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
